d\": \"Tether Georgia GELT\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Tether and Government of Georgia Plan Lari-Backed Stablecoin 'GELT' Under New Regulatory Framework\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Tether\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"tether\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"CryptoFox\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/tether-georgia-government-plan-lari-backed-stablecoin-gelt\",\n    \"https://cryptofox.news/news/tether-and-georgia-partner-to-launch-gel-stablecoin-for-national-lari\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Tether and the government of Georgia announced a partnership to launch a stablecoin called GELT, pegged to the Georgian lari.\",\n    \"The initiative leverages the National Bank of Georgia's new virtual asset service providers (VASP) regulatory framework released on March 6, which requires full reserve backing, offering documents, and external auditor verification.\",\n    \"The GELT stablecoin is aimed at supporting cross-border commerce and digital payments in Georgia, with its technical deployment and rollout supported by Tether Operations Limited.\",\n    \"GELT joins Tether's non-USD sovereign-linked stablecoin lineup, which has historically included pegs for the Mexican peso (MXNT), offshore Chinese yuan (CNHT), and a planned United Arab Emirates dirham stablecoin.\",\n    \"Key officials backing the project include Georgian Prime Minister Irakli Kobakhidze and National Bank of Georgia Governor Natia Turnava, who emphasized that the collaboration will enhance transaction transparency and digital financial infrastructure.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"# Tether, Georgia plan lari-backed stablecoin GELT under new rules\\n\\nGeorgia’s central bank released stablecoin rules in March covering reserve backing, issuer documents and external auditor verification.\\n\\nStablecoin issuer Tether and the government of Georgia plan to launch a stablecoin called “GELT” that would represent the Georgian lari under the country’s digital asset regulatory framework.\\n\\nOn Monday, Tether said the stablecoin is expected to support cross-border commerce and digital payments in Georgia. The company said GELT's structure, rollout and regulatory implementation will be announced at a later stage.\\n\\nThe plan builds on Georgia’s recent efforts to develop rules for digital assets and stablecoins, including a framework covering reserve management, redemption rights, issuer oversight and Anti-Money Laundering compliance. In March, the National Bank of Georgia said it had developed rules for the initial offering of “stable virtual assets,” including requirements for full reserve backing, offering documents and external auditor verification.\\n\\nGeorgian Prime Minister Irakli Kobakhidze said the partnership with Tether would help lay the foundations for a more connected and transparent financial world. National Bank of Georgia President Natia Turnava said the central bank welcomes the collaboration as part of its strategy to advance digital financial infrastructure.\\n\\nThe announcement did not say who would legally issue GELT, where reserves would be held, or whether holders would have direct redemption rights. The company also did not provide a definite launch timeline.\\n\\nTether acknowledged Cointelegraph’s request for comment. Cointelegraph reached out to the National Bank of Georgia for more information, but did not receive a response by publication.\\n\\n## Georgia released stablecoin rules in March\\n\\nOn March 6, the National Bank of Georgia released rules covering stablecoin issuance. The framework said a stablecoin offering in Georgia cannot be provided without prior written consent from the National Bank.\\n\\nIt applies to virtual asset service providers, or VASPs, registered with the central bank, while companies that are not registered as VASPs must obtain registration before conducting a stablecoin offering or providing related services. The central bank said stablecoins in circulation must be fully backed by reserve assets that meet liquidity and credit quality requirements.\\n\\nTether, the issuer of the world’s largest stablecoin by market capitalization, has officially entered into a strategic partnership with the Government of Georgia to launch GEL₮, a digital asset pegged to the Georgian Lari. This initiative represents a significant milestone in the integration of traditional national currencies with blockchain technology. The project operates under a newly established regulatory framework designed specifically for digital assets, positioning Georgia as a pioneer in the adoption of sovereign-linked stablecoins within the global financial ecosystem.\\n\\n## Strategic Integration of the Georgian Lari\\n\\nThe collaboration aims to modernize Georgia's financial infrastructure by placing the GEL (Georgian Lari) on digital asset rails. This move is expected to enhance transaction transparency and speed while reducing costs for domestic and international transfers. According to the official announcement, the development of GEL₮ is supported by the National Bank of Georgia, ensuring that the digital token adheres to strict monetary standards and compliance protocols.\\n\\nWith visionary partners like Tether, Georgia is laying the groundwork for a more connected, transparent, and digitally empowered financial world, stated Georgian Prime Minister Irakli Kobakhidze, emphasizing the nation's commitment to digital transformation.\\n\\n## Regulatory Framework and Global Impact\\n\\nThe launch of GEL₮ is not merely a technical deployment but a result of extensive legal preparation. Tether CEO Paolo Ardoino noted that stablecoins are evolving into a fundamental component of global financial architecture. He praised the Georgian authorities for their proactive approach in creating a robust regulatory environment.\\n\\nThe partnership involves several key stakeholders:\\n- The National Bank of Georgia, led by Governor Natia Turnava, providing oversight and institutional support.\\n- Tether Operations Limited, supplying the technical expertise and liquidity management infrastructure.\\n- Georgian Parliament members, including Vakhtang Turnava, who have facilitated the legislative transition for digital assets.\\n\\nBy establishing these guidelines, Georgia aims to attract further investment from the decentralized finance (DeFi) sector and fintech developers.\\n\\n## Conclusion\\n\\nThe introduction of GEL₮ marks a pivotal moment for both Tether and the Georgian state, demonstrating how private technology providers and public institutions can collaborate on Web3 initiatives. As the project rolls out, it will serve as a case study for other nations looking to tokenize their national currencies under a regulated, secure, and transparent framework. This development reinforces the growing trend of stablecoins acting as a bridge between legacy banking systems and the emerging digital economy.\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779717006785}}
